          %p
            then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            inherits a lax action of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℳ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}
            and is hence a graded monad. Every lax action can be generated from a strict action in this way. Initial and terminal such resolutions of a lax action then generalize the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo ≅
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M} \cong 1
            situation in which is a monad is resolved into adjunctions with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category"} Kleisli
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Moore+category"} Eilenberg-Moore
            categories (
            %a{:href => "#FKM"}> FKM 16
            ).
          %h2#uses Uses
          %p
            Graded monads can be used to construct ordinary monads by left Kan extension in the 2-category of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+monoidal+functor"} lax monoidal functors
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+natural+transformation"} monoidal transformations
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#FPKan"} Fritz &amp; Perrone 18, Theorem 2.1
          %p
            For example, taking the left Kan extension of the graded list monad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Set
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B} \mathbb{N} \to [Set,Set]
            described above results in the usual list monad on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Set
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \to [Set,Set]
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#FPKant"} Fritz &amp; Perrone 17
            = succeed "," do
              %em Kantorovich monad
          %p
            A
            %em useful feature
            of such constructions is that the multiplication of the graded monad is often a
            %em strong
            monoidal functor in practice. For the graded list monad, this is because a list of length
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m n
            can be decomposed uniquely into a list of length
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            of lists of length
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi m
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi n
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                      %mi n
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_m T_n X \longrightarrow T_{m n} X
            is an isomorphism. For the probability monad mentioned in the previous paragraph, the analogous phenomenon occurs as well, and this can be exploited e.g. in order to prove a disintegration theorem for finite first moment Radon probability measures on complete metric spaces (
            %a{:href => "#Perrone"}> Perrone, Theorem 2.6.9
            ).
